{"compressedText":"# 72-Hour Power Outage: Home Prep\n\nPrepare an ordinary household to manage safely for up to 72 hours. Temperate climate, no backup generator assumed. Do the sections in order; earlier ones matter most.\n\n## Water\n- Store 4 litres per person per day = 12 litres per person for 72 hours; half drinking, half cooking and basic hygiene.\n- Food-grade containers, out of direct sunlight, replace every 6 months.\n- If a storm is forecast with warning, also fill the bathtub — for flushing toilets and washing, never drinking.\n\n## Food\n- Dedicated shelf of non-perishable food for 3 days: canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, shelf-stable milk.\n- Pick no-cook foods; own a manual can opener (an electric one is useless).\n- Eat in order: perishable refrigerator food, then freezer, then emergency shelf.\n\n## Refrigerator and freezer\nHolding times, doors kept closed:\n\n| Item | Stays safely cold |\n|---|---|\n| Unopened refrigerator | about 4 hours |\n| Full freezer | about 48 hours |\n| Half-full freezer | about 24 hours |\n\n- Keep the freezer consolidated and full, even if some space holds containers of frozen water. Keep both doors closed as much as possible; tape a reminder note to each.\n- Discard any perishable food more than 2 hours above 4°C; when in doubt, throw it out.\n\n## Light\n- Battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as primary light; avoid candles entirely — a leading cause of house fires during outages.\n- At least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries per light.\n- One headlamp beside each bed, one lantern in the kitchen, placed to be found in the dark.\n\n## Heat and safety\n- Never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window: each produces carbon monoxide — odourless and potentially fatal.\n- At least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep.\n- In cold weather, choose one small room on a south-facing side, close its door, and gather everyone there; layered clothing and blankets beat any improvised heater.\n- If the indoor temperature falls below 10°C and you cannot stay warm, relocate to a warming centre or a neighbour's home.\n\n## Communication and information\n- Battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts; know your local emergency station's frequency beforehand.\n- Charge every phone and power bank whenever a storm is forecast; a full modern 20,000 mAh power bank recharges a typical phone roughly 4 times.\n- Put your phone in low-power mode at the outage's start.\n- Agree in advance on one out-of-area contact every household member texts; text messages get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls.\n\n## Medical needs\n- First-aid kit and a seven-day supply of every prescription medication, rotated to stay in date.\n- Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally stays usable at room temperature up to 28 days — but confirm your specific medication with a pharmacist beforehand.\n- Write your pharmacy, doctor, and poison-control phone numbers on paper; a dead phone is not a phone book.\n\n## When the power returns\n- Wait 5 minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise.\n- Check the freezer: food still containing ice crystals can be safely refrozen.\n- Restock everything used within one week, starting with water and batteries.\n- Write a one-page note on what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one gap before the next outage.","expectedMetric":583,"hardConstraintNote":"Remains a fully self-contained instruction document: it keeps the title, all eight topic sections, and every actionable directive in executable order (prepare -> water -> food -> cold storage -> light -> heat/safety -> comms -> medical -> recovery). A reader needs nothing external to act; the bullets and table are directives, not references. No quantity, duration, temperature, item, or ordered step was removed, so the held-out comprehension gate has the same fact surface to draw from.","killedLeverCitations":[],"lens":"restructurer","leverId":"restructure-lists-and-timing-table","rationale":"The original is continuous expository prose padded with connective framing ('because', 'so that', 'since', 'which means'). As restructurer I collapse the intro to two sentences, convert every section body into terse imperative bullets, and lift the three refrigerator/freezer timings into a table so the ~4h / ~48h / ~24h facts stand as scannable rows instead of a compound sentence. Numeric facts are kept but rendered in symbol form (4°C, 10°C, 20,000 mAh, 4 litres = 12 litres) which drops filler tokens without dropping any quantity. No fact is deleted: all six food items, all three timings, both temperatures, the 6-month/28-day/7-day/5-minute/one-week durations, the CO-alarm and candle-fire warnings, and the full eat-order and restock-order sequences survive. Result is 583 words vs the 730 baseline (frontier.json), a ~20% cut that clears the line-editor ceiling (~615) the frontier priced.","title":"Restructure prose into imperative bullet lists with a cold-holding timing table"}